From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 14:56:41 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 513/513] [DS4.1] Sparse-MLA page tables over each row's live
 pages

At --max-model-len 1M and 256-token blocks the decode metadata wrote every
one of a row's 4,096 page-table columns, 32 loop iterations a thread in
12 CTAs at a decode step (15 us of a 6-row call), and 268 MB a layer at a
16K-row prefill chunk (483 us a call). A row's readers reach only the
pages of its visible entries: the sparse MLA's kept lanes and the
indexer's tiles look up positions below the row's visible length, and a
masked lane's lookup is clamped into the table and discarded.

decode_metadata and pages now write those columns alone (ceil(visible /
page), at most the table's width; none for a row without a request), and
leave the rest of the buffer as it was. Profiled ceiling (request
inventory 5, TP4 ring, rank 0): metadata_decode is 0.70% of a c1 decode
step, 0.41% at c8 and 1.20% of a 15K-token prefill; metadata_pages 0.10%
of a c1 step.

Tests: both kernels equal the torch references on every live column and
leave the others untouched; the indexer (dense and candidates) and every sparse-MLA
route keep their bits with every dead column poisoned and the masked lanes
pointed into them. benchmark_deepseek_v41_metadata_pages.py times both
against the replaced kernels (kept as they were at 26382f5cf), gated. The compressor
test's page-table check passes its visible lengths, every column live.
